{"text": "STATE OF MARYLAND\n\nDHMH\n\nMaryland Department of Health and Mental Hygiene\n201 W. Preston Street + Baltimore, Maryland 21201\nMarin O'Malley, Govemor~ Anthony G, Brown, Lt Govemor~ Joshua M. Shatin, M.D, Secretary\n\nJanuary 21, 2014\n\n\u2018The Honorable John Delaney\nU.S. House of Representatives\n1632 Longworth House Office Building\nWashington, DC 20515\n\nDear Congressman Delaney,\n\n\u2018Thank you for your letter of January 6. Governor O\u2019Malley is focused on enrolling as many Marylanders as\npossible in health coverage, both before March 31 and beyond. The Governor recently announced that Maryland\ndoes not intend to partner with the federal facilitated marketplace (FFM) during the current open enrollment period,\nwhich ends March 31. The Governor made this decision because the risks of such a partnership, including the risks\nofa transition at this time, outweigh the potential benefits.\n\nKey considerations included:\n\u2018\u00a9 the need to build and test a database with an effective interface with the Medicaid Management Information\nSystem, such that enrollment data can pass effectively from the FFM to the database to the state\u2019s Medicaid\nsystem;\n\n\u2018\u00a9 the requirement that carriers upload and assure the accuracy of plan data, as well as take other steps to\nreceive enrollment data from the FFM; and\n\n\u2018\u00a9 the challenge of transition for many Marylanders who would have started an application in the current\nenrollment system.\n\nOf particular note, CareFirst CEO Chet Burrell strongly recommended that the state continue making progress with\n\u2018our current system, rather than switch to a partnership with the FFM, at this time, He wrote that \u201ca move to the\nFederal Exchange at this time would present substantial new risks and costs\u201d and recommended that the state \u201cfix as\n\u2018much as it can with its own Exchange during this current open enrollment period.\u201d We found Mr. Burrell\u2019s position\nparticularly relevant both (1) because his company is familiar with both the FFM and the Maryland Health Benefit\nExchange because it operates in Virginia and Maryland; and (2) CareFirst\u2019s interest is in enroiling as many people\nas possible, as quickly as possible.\n\n\u2018We will continue to provide updates on our work with Maryland Health Connection, both before and after the close\nof open enrollment this year.\n\n\u2018Thank you for your interest in this matter.\nSincerely,\n\nDye ae Ma\n\nJoshua M. Sharfstei\nSecretary\n\n\u2018Toll Free -877-4MD-DHMH \u2014 TTY /Maryand Relay Service 1-800-735.2258\nWeb Site: sew dhmh maryland gov",
